✅ 24.Monitor Locks

24.1 Definition

A Monitor is an internal synchronization mechanism provided by the JVM.

It ensures that:
🔴 Only one thread can execute synchronized code protected by that monitor at a time.
🔴 Other threads wait until the monitor becomes available.

Think of a monitor as the lock (intrinsic lock) attached to every Java object.

24.2 Does Every Object Have a Monitor?

YES.
Every object created in Java automatically has an associated monitor.

Example

Object obj = new Object();
String s = "Hello";
Integer x = 10;
Counter counter = new Counter();

Each object has its own monitor.

Object A
   ↓
Monitor A

Object B
   ↓
Monitor B

Object C
   ↓
Monitor C

This is why we can write

synchronized(obj) { }
synchronized(counter) { }
synchronized(this) { }


24.3 Why Do We Need a Monitor?

Suppose two threads execute
count++;

simultaneously.
Without a monitor

Thread-A
↓
count++
↑
Thread-B

Both modify the same data simultaneously.

Result

❌ Race Condition
With a monitor

Monitor
↓
Thread-A
↓
Critical Section
↓
Thread-B Waits

Only one thread enters.

24.4 Internal Working of synchronized

Suppose

synchronized(lock){
    count++;
}

Internally

Acquire Monitor
↓
Execute Code
↓
Release Monitor

The JVM performs these steps automatically.

24.5 Example

class Counter {
    private int count = 0;
    public void increment(){
        synchronized(this){
            count++;
        }
    }
}

Question

Which monitor is acquired?
Answer Counter Object's Monitor


24.6 Monitor Ownership

A monitor always has at most one owner.

Example

Counter Monitor
↓
Owner
↓
Thread-A

Question

What happens if Thread-B arrives?

Answer
BLOCKED
because Thread-A owns the monitor.

